[{:seon.task/name "writes-commit-before-any-wait"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit C #8, the owner's question \"why commit every 18?\": an agent's run-ahead writes commit when it is idle (agent.clj:61), at its next transact! once the 10 ms window passed (:126), and before a merge, a clone or the attempt's end (:206, :436, turn.clj:71). A Seon agent's whole attempt runs inside one eval (seon.turn/work!), so it is never idle: a turn's writes stay uncommitted through the next model call, seconds to minutes, and commit only at the next turn's first write (real3: 19 commits over 335 evals on task-t5-edit-forms). A crash or kill in that window loses exactly the record of what the agent just did, and it breaks the durability law (a write followed by an effect outside the database waits for its flush; a model call is such an effect). Commit before any blocking wait: agent/fx! (every effect), run-tests-forked, await-op!. Then every turn is durable before the agent waits on the outside world. Seen by lane THREADS (2026-10-21): bin/check durable-read fails about 1 run in 3, on main's binary too (2 of 6) and on jolt/threads (1 of 3): the clone :a4 answers its transact! (count 1), root SIGKILLs itself and its process group at once, and after recovery :dur-clone holds no note (seon.check/durable-write, check.clj:712; read at :756). An answered write that crosses a process (the reply to root) must be committed and flushed first; this task's proof should include durable-read 10 runs in a row."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-turn-is-committed-before-its-model-call}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-process-says-what-it-is"
  :seon.task/plan "In Activity Monitor and ps, every process a root forks (the fork server, its standby, each agent, clone and test child) shows the root's own command line, ./seon root PORT, so the owner can't tell a root from an agent or which checkout it belongs to (2026-10-21: \"I'm seeing spammed seon processes again\"; 14 processes across five checkouts, all named alike). Each process names itself by role and checkout when it starts, e.g. \"seon fork-server (next-wiki)\", \"seon agent a3 (next-wiki)\": on macOS by rewriting its argv area in place (what setproctitle does), so ps shows it; and the same name is a fact on its entity. Part of the process pillar (AGENTS). Proof: after spawning an agent, ps -o command= for each pid names its role."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-death-names-its-cause"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-21 on the standing root: attempts `seon.fences/one-test-bound-derived-and-named` (agent a5) and `seon.fences/a-transient-model-failure-parks-r1-test` (agent a9) ended `:closed` with reason \"agent a9 died\" and nothing more, while root's log held the causes: an uncaught `{:type :fatal-commit-error}` from Datahike in `seon.repl/record!`, and a ClassCastException (nil cannot be cast to IFn) in `seon.agent/run-tests-forked`, plausibly after the agent redefined a platform function its own loop calls (a real REPL may; the merge judges). The next attempt and root learn nothing from a bare death. Correct: the agent process's entrant boundary (`seon.agent`'s loop, `seon.fork-server/main` for a child) writes the uncaught exception as the attempt's end, the one error map (`:seon.error/type`, `:seon.error/operation`, message, the cause chain) on its branch, as any other end is written, before the process exits; root's judge carries it to the attempt's reason and evidence, so the refinement's design step reads why. Then find the fatal commit's cause from that evidence."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agent-that-throws-ends-with-its-cause}} {:seon.task/name "an-agent-holds-mains-tests"
  :seon.task/plan "Audit 2026-10-22 (notes/AUDIT-2026-10-22-agents.md, broken thing 1): 263 of 265 agent contexts showed every proof `:no-assertions`. Cause: `seon.program/held?` treated every form imported from the source tree (`:form/source`) as compiled into the binary, so `seon.program/load-changes!` (root's writer, `seon.fork-server/server-step` :tx) never loaded test/'s namespaces, which are imported but not built in; agents fork from the fork server and held no test namespace. Fix: `held?` is \"this process holds the form's namespace\". That exposed a hang: the fork server answered neither :stats nor :fork after loading test/, bisected to seon.merge-test, whose top-level def opened the test store (`seon.db/test-db`, d/connect) at load; in the fork server (one thread, none of root's pool threads) the connect never returns. A test namespace loads without effects: that value is a function now. Holding every test, an agent's run selects what the done rule (`seon.reconcile/done-refusals`) reads: `seon.agent/test-vars`, the task's accumulated proofs (root's :tests request names the agent's task) and `seon.program/reaching-tests` over `seon.program/changed-vars` (what the branch changed since the main it forked from), never main's whole suite."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/an-agent-holds-mains-tests-and-runs-them seon.agent-test/an-agents-test-run-is-its-proofs-and-what-its-change-reaches seon.merge-test/declared-data-merges-by-key}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-wide-reach-stays-cheap"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-10-22 by lane SELECT: an agent's test run is its task's proofs plus `seon.program/reaching-tests` over what its branch changed (`seon.agent/test-vars`); a one-defn change reaches 1 test (9.9 ms the whole forked run), but a change to `seon.config/value` reaches 263 tests (359 ms to walk) and to `seon.print/text` 252, root-booting tests included, all run in the agent's fork. Honest reach, the next cost: measure what such a run costs end to end and make it proportional to what changed (results reused by reach digest, `seon.program/tests-run-by-reach`, which the agent's run does not use yet)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-wide-reach-reuses-unchanged-results}}]
